        "uncertainties_disclosed": false
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "καὶ εἰς τέλος κολασθησομένας καθιστᾶσιν τῶν ἀνακεκραμένων τὰς ἠπατημένας αὐτοῖς ψυχάς, ὡς ἔφαμεν, εἰς τὸ καθάρσιον χωρήσαντες πῦρ.",
          "english_quote": "And, having entered the purifying fire, as we said, they bring it about that the deceived souls of those who have been mingled with them will be punished to the end.",
          "issue": "The draft moves both the fire-entry clause and “as we said” ahead of the punishment statement. The Greek places the punishment statement first, followed by “as we said” and then the fire-entry clause. Besides violating the required clause order, the rearrangement changes the apparent attachment of the retrospective remark. Taking χωρήσαντες with the demons is defensible and already explained; that interpretation is not itself a block.",
          "suggested_revision": "Keep the punishment statement first and the fire-entry clause last. One candidate retaining the draft’s subject interpretation is: “And they bring it about that the deceived souls of those who have been mingled with them will be punished to the end, as we said, after they—the demons—have entered the purifying fire.” Retain the existing subject-ambiguity note and submit the replacement for fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "τῶν ἀνακεκραμένων τὰς ἠπατημένας αὐτοῖς ψυχάς",
          "english_quote": "the deceived souls of those who have been mingled with them",
          "issue": "The English assigns αὐτοῖς to the mingling, but its position also naturally supports attachment to the perfect passive participle ἠπατημένας: “the souls deceived by them.” The longer-distance attachment adopted by the draft is defensible, particularly in light of the preceding discussion of blending with souls, but this material attachment alternative and the reason for choosing between them are not disclosed.",
          "suggested_revision": "Add a source-quoted note explaining that the main text takes αὐτοῖς with ἀνακεκραμένων, supported by the blending described in 9.11, while an alternative is “the souls deceived by them, belonging to those who have been mingled.” No correction to the printed Greek is required. Any resulting English revision must receive fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
